<h2>The case</h2>
<p>The petitioner sought anticipatory bail — under Section 438 CrPC, corresponding to Section 482 of the BNSS — in connection with NDPS Case No. 20 of 2026, arising out of Gangarampur P.S. Case No. 169 of 2026 dated 27 March 2026, registered under Sections 21(c), 22(c), 23(c), 27A, 28 and 29 of the Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985. Those provisions span possession and transport of commercial quantities, financing of illicit traffic, attempts and conspiracy — on paper, the gravest end of the NDPS spectrum.</p>
<p>The petitioner&rsquo;s position, accepted as the operative premise of the order, was narrow and factual: nothing was recovered from him, and he stood implicated only on the statements of co-accused persons. The chargesheet had already been filed. The State opposed the plea on the strength of the case diary.</p>

<h2>What the Court did</h2>
<p>Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Partha Sarathi Chatterjee granted anticipatory bail. In the event of arrest, the petitioner is to be released on a bond of Rs. 20,000 with two sureties of like amount, one of whom must be local, subject to conditions: attendance before the trial court on every date, and no movement out of the police station&rsquo;s jurisdiction without the trial court&rsquo;s permission. The application was disposed of accordingly.</p>

<h2>The architecture of the decision</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Source of implication.</strong> Co-accused statements alone — no recovery from the petitioner, no independent corroboration recorded in the order.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Stage of the case.</strong> Chargesheet filed: the investigative justification for custody had largely exhausted itself.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Calibrated conditions.</strong> Local surety, trial-date attendance and a travel restriction keep the petitioner tethered to the proceeding.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No merits forecast.</strong> As always at this stage, the order decides custody, not culpability; the trial will test the prosecution&rsquo;s material in full.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider point for NDPS defence work</h2>
<p>The NDPS Act&rsquo;s severity makes precision about the accused-specific material essential. The Act&rsquo;s presumptions and the rigours of Section 37 attach their full force to persons found in possession or otherwise directly connected to the contraband; they do not convert every name mentioned in a co-accused&rsquo;s statement into a fit case for custody. Defence counsel should therefore force the record to answer one question: what, apart from the words of persons already in the net, connects this client to the offence? Where the honest answer is nothing — no recovery, no CDR linkage, no financial trail — the case for pre-arrest protection is at its strongest, particularly once the chargesheet shows the investigation closed without that material surfacing.</p>
<p>For investigating agencies, the same order is a quiet prescription: corroborate before you implicate. A prosecution built on co-accused statements alone begins the trial carrying a structural weakness that no amount of custody can repair.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information and does not comment on any pending investigation or trial.</div>
